WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.837 --> 00:00:06.170
GURMEET GOINDI: Welcome friends.

00:00:06.170 --> 00:00:07.630
My name is Gurmeet Goindi.

00:00:07.630 --> 00:00:08.710
I go by GG.

00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:13.070
I'm a product manager on
Google database's product team.

00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:15.760
Today I'll be talking
about Bare Metal Solutions.

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:19.750
And how Bare Metal solutions
enable all our enterprises

00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:23.200
to bring all their
workloads closer to GCP,

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:25.170
regardless if
their workloads are

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:29.080
written for a collaborative
environment or not.

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:33.970
As you know most of our
enterprises that we work with

00:00:33.970 --> 00:00:38.800
are planning to move to a
public cloud environment.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:43.520
Not only that, Gartner surveyed
a lot of these enterprises.

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:47.890
And they came to a conclusion
that 80% of these enterprises

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:53.200
will move out of their own
operating data centers by 2025.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:55.690
A lot of you are
already in the journey

00:00:55.690 --> 00:00:58.750
of moving out of your
current infrastructure

00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:03.610
to a cloud-based environment,
either in a public cloud

00:01:03.610 --> 00:01:07.390
setting or in a
private cloud setting.

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:10.450
What are the key migration
and modernization

00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:13.750
challenges that most of our
enterprise customers face?

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:18.970
First and foremost, owning and
operating the infrastructure

00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:22.820
was a big differentiator
for a lot of the enterprises

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:24.400
20 years ago.

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:27.430
But given the advances that
we have in current silicon

00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:31.860
technology, whether it's
computer storage or networking,

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:35.050
the differentiation owning and
operating your infrastructure

00:01:35.050 --> 00:01:38.050
brings in is fairly
low to your business.

00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:41.470
Secondly, most enterprises
want to embrace

00:01:41.470 --> 00:01:44.580
the mythology of developing
applications in cloud,

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:48.370
whether it would be agile
development models, whether it

00:01:48.370 --> 00:01:50.410
would be absorbing
new technologies

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:55.480
like artificial intelligence
and AML at a much quicker pace,

00:01:55.480 --> 00:01:59.440
or whether it be adopting a very
different way, a DevOps- driven

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:02.690
way of running IT.

00:02:02.690 --> 00:02:05.050
But where the
complexity comes in

00:02:05.050 --> 00:02:09.400
is how do you marry these
two different concepts?

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:13.610
And this is where
Google Cloud steps in.

00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:15.860
Our goal is to
meet you where you

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:20.480
are to be a partner in creating
a migration strategy that

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:26.040
lets you run what you want,
where you want, how you want.

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:30.620
Take your investment
and your current assets

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:35.330
and gradually roll them out
into the best available platform

00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:38.890
for them in a public cloud.

00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:41.590
Our goal here is to
reduce your risk,

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:46.090
reduce your cost, at the same
time maintaining an agility

00:02:46.090 --> 00:02:49.720
to bring your mission
critical workloads as close as

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:53.870
to Google Cloud and
modernize from there on.

00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:56.340
We believe this to be
a three step journey.

00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:59.590
First, all the
workloads that you

00:02:59.590 --> 00:03:01.630
run today which are
not written to run

00:03:01.630 --> 00:03:05.850
in the cloud-native
environment, we move them as-is.

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:08.550
By moving them as-is,
we reduce our risk

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:12.160
it takes to come running near
a public cloud environment.

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:16.320
Second, we also
divert your investment

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:20.400
in rather retuning your
existing applications

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:22.920
to run with existing
legacy applications

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:25.770
to run in a public
line environment.

00:03:25.770 --> 00:03:28.290
Rather taking that
investment to modernize

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:30.760
those applications to
run natively of the cloud

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:32.290
environment.

00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:35.140
Third, in this approach
we believe not only

00:03:35.140 --> 00:03:38.890
you can do this at a much
reduced cost with a much

00:03:38.890 --> 00:03:41.920
reduced risk, but
at the same time,

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:46.430
we can speed up your journey
to migration to cloud.

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:50.520
And each application will
have its own unique journey.

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:53.220
Each application has its
unique business value

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:56.210
to you as a customer,
and we understand that.

00:03:56.210 --> 00:03:59.490
We are here to support the
unique journey per application.

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:02.130
Some of them will lift
and ship from one premise

00:04:02.130 --> 00:04:03.930
and stay in cloud as-is.

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:06.660
Some of them will have
to modernize before they

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:08.040
can be lift and ship.

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:10.440
Some of them might
have to be rewritten

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:12.270
in a Cloud-native format.

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:16.079
Google Cloud provides
options for all these parts

00:04:16.079 --> 00:04:21.899
with your business goals
being central in our minds.

00:04:21.899 --> 00:04:26.250
Let's see how Bare Metal
Solutions play a role

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:28.070
in solving these problems.

00:04:28.070 --> 00:04:31.410
But before we go there,
let's try to characterize

00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:33.360
what those challenges are.

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:37.470
First, majority of your
mission critical applications

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:44.400
run on [INAUDIBLE] on databases
that are not natively written

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:45.770
for Cloud.

00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:48.560
At the same time,
they are the pieces

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:50.870
of applications that run
the core of your business.

00:04:50.870 --> 00:04:55.950
They have a very high impact on
how your business runs today.

00:04:55.950 --> 00:05:00.180
Second, they are really complex
in their own requirements.

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:02.210
They have been written
over the years.

00:05:02.210 --> 00:05:04.650
They have a plethora
of combinations

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:08.550
of off-the-shelves apps
and the apps that work.

00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:10.710
They didn't have
homegrown apps that

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:14.040
have contributions of
decades by your engineers

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:15.690
and other engineers.

00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:19.650
So migrating such apps or
[? re-migrating ?] these apps

00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:21.840
need careful attention.

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:25.110
And that's where
Google Cloud comes in.

00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:27.330
In addition, some
of these apps also

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:29.610
might have very stringent
hardware requirements,

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:32.910
the type of hardware that
the particular software

00:05:32.910 --> 00:05:34.050
vendors will certify.

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:36.390
And these apps will have
to run on those hardware.

00:05:36.390 --> 00:05:39.300
And here, also, Bare Metal
comes in and solves that problem

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:43.230
by providing a certified
hardware to any applications.

00:05:43.230 --> 00:05:47.700
But what's the crux
of this challenge

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:51.660
is the interoperability
between your various databases,

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:56.100
various materials where
applications is also

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:57.240
a challenge.

00:05:57.240 --> 00:05:59.040
In many cases, a
lot of our customers

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:03.990
don't even have a full
view of how intertwined

00:06:03.990 --> 00:06:07.680
their application estate is
with the databases estate.

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:11.610
We cannot modernize just one
without also paying careful

00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:14.770
detail attention to the other.

00:06:14.770 --> 00:06:16.840
With this [INAUDIBLE]
kept in mind,

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:19.150
we developed Bare
Metal Solutions,

00:06:19.150 --> 00:06:22.210
which provides you
the best platform

00:06:22.210 --> 00:06:25.330
to migrate all your
legacy and complex apps,

00:06:25.330 --> 00:06:27.760
which are difficult
to modernize, as close

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:28.570
as to Cloud.

00:06:28.570 --> 00:06:32.290
And then, on a step by
step, gradual manner,

00:06:32.290 --> 00:06:35.830
take these applications and
migrate them and modernize them

00:06:35.830 --> 00:06:39.710
in a Cloud-native environment
wherever it's possible.

00:06:39.710 --> 00:06:43.890
Let's see what Google Bare
Metal Solution offers you.

00:06:43.890 --> 00:06:49.030
The key context of
Bare Metal Solutions

00:06:49.030 --> 00:06:53.220
is our ability to offer
purpose-built hardware.

00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:56.880
A lot of software vendors and a
lot of operating system vendors

00:06:56.880 --> 00:07:00.520
have restrictions on
the kind of hardware

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:03.330
their applications can run
on, the kind of hardware that

00:07:03.330 --> 00:07:07.050
gives them the best maximum
performance for their databases

00:07:07.050 --> 00:07:08.530
of applications.

00:07:08.530 --> 00:07:11.070
So what we have done is taken
the latest and the greatest

00:07:11.070 --> 00:07:13.140
the silicon industry
has to offer,

00:07:13.140 --> 00:07:16.830
married it to what a
certified soft hardware

00:07:16.830 --> 00:07:19.140
list for these specialized
vendors would be,

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:21.430
and providing do
you in a setting

00:07:21.430 --> 00:07:25.740
which is very close to GCP,
from a latency standpoint.

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:28.500
And you have all
access to all your GCP

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:33.400
services from this purpose-built
hardware solution and vice

00:07:33.400 --> 00:07:37.060
versa so that you can move the
data freely between the two.

00:07:37.060 --> 00:07:39.250
At the same time,
your applications

00:07:39.250 --> 00:07:41.390
that are running in
one way or the other

00:07:41.390 --> 00:07:44.380
do not see significant
network latency difference

00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:47.140
between the two, as well.

00:07:47.140 --> 00:07:53.050
We wrap this around a
Google-managed billing

00:07:53.050 --> 00:07:54.340
and support services.

00:07:54.340 --> 00:07:56.950
At the same time you
contract with Google

00:07:56.950 --> 00:07:59.740
for all your Bare
Metal needs, Google

00:07:59.740 --> 00:08:02.140
is the only party
you engage with.

00:08:02.140 --> 00:08:07.720
Google, in turn, procures
the latest indicators

00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:09.640
the hardware
industry has to offer

00:08:09.640 --> 00:08:12.710
and provides that in the
Bare Metal Solution for you.

00:08:12.710 --> 00:08:16.360
But you might ask, what
happens to all the investment

00:08:16.360 --> 00:08:17.590
that you have already made?

00:08:17.590 --> 00:08:20.510
Your existing database
administrators team,

00:08:20.510 --> 00:08:22.450
system administrator teams--

00:08:22.450 --> 00:08:25.390
what happens to all the
system integrators you have

00:08:25.390 --> 00:08:27.910
been working on over the years?

00:08:27.910 --> 00:08:31.450
You can bring them as-is
to this environment.

00:08:31.450 --> 00:08:33.429
For most [INAUDIBLE],,
this environment

00:08:33.429 --> 00:08:35.260
will look like a
new hardware refresh

00:08:35.260 --> 00:08:36.909
to what you're already doing.

00:08:36.909 --> 00:08:39.850
And you can pick and choose
the pieces of infrastructure

00:08:39.850 --> 00:08:42.610
that you need to modernize,
move to Cloud-native

00:08:42.610 --> 00:08:44.920
or keep them in Bare
Metal as your application

00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:47.650
needs dictates.

00:08:47.650 --> 00:08:50.870
Most techie and tech
enthusiasts will say,

00:08:50.870 --> 00:08:54.040
I will only believe this when
I see architecture diagrams.

00:08:54.040 --> 00:08:57.040
I wanted to walk you through
what architecture looks like

00:08:57.040 --> 00:08:59.530
and also open the
possibilities of what

00:08:59.530 --> 00:09:01.360
you can do with
this architecture,

00:09:01.360 --> 00:09:04.330
and how you can bring in some
of the most complex workloads

00:09:04.330 --> 00:09:06.920
you run with this architecture.

00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:08.120
Two aspects of this.

00:09:08.120 --> 00:09:11.130
On the right side, what you
see is a region extension.

00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:14.240
This is a dedicated
hardware hosting

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:17.435
space which is connected with
low latency interconnected

00:09:17.435 --> 00:09:20.150
to Google Cloud.

00:09:20.150 --> 00:09:24.230
Every entity in Google Cloud
is able to access every entity

00:09:24.230 --> 00:09:25.870
in the region extension.

00:09:25.870 --> 00:09:30.290
But latency is, in most cases,
less than 1 millisecond.

00:09:30.290 --> 00:09:33.653
In some cases, less
than 2 milliseconds.

00:09:33.653 --> 00:09:36.320
From an application perspective,
or from a database perspective,

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:38.720
or from a [INAUDIBLE]
perspective, this

00:09:38.720 --> 00:09:43.430
all exist and sits in one
large networking construct.

00:09:43.430 --> 00:09:47.510
And every application has access
to every other entity running

00:09:47.510 --> 00:09:49.850
in this extension.

00:09:49.850 --> 00:09:52.030
The three common design
patterns that you see,

00:09:52.030 --> 00:09:54.610
first, is in some
cases, customers

00:09:54.610 --> 00:09:56.740
wants to run their
packaged apps which

00:09:56.740 --> 00:09:59.050
only runs on certain
certified hardware

00:09:59.050 --> 00:10:01.330
directly on a bandwidth server.

00:10:01.330 --> 00:10:04.360
Second, our customers have
been bringing their database

00:10:04.360 --> 00:10:06.940
and keep their
applications in GCP.

00:10:06.940 --> 00:10:09.610
And the database comes with
their own internal tooling,

00:10:09.610 --> 00:10:13.360
their own internal monitoring,
their own internal best

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:14.260
practices.

00:10:14.260 --> 00:10:17.650
They can bring the internal
tooling and the database as-is

00:10:17.650 --> 00:10:21.100
under Bare Metal servers,
and connect seamlessly

00:10:21.100 --> 00:10:24.565
to the applications
running in these.

00:10:24.565 --> 00:10:26.440
The third design pattern
you see the customer

00:10:26.440 --> 00:10:28.280
just comes with the database.

00:10:28.280 --> 00:10:31.000
In certain areas, we provide
an automation tool package,

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:33.220
which makes the
lifecycle of managing

00:10:33.220 --> 00:10:35.200
a database much easier.

00:10:35.200 --> 00:10:38.590
This automated tool pack is
an open source contribution

00:10:38.590 --> 00:10:39.350
from Google.

00:10:39.350 --> 00:10:40.690
You can find that on GitHub.

00:10:40.690 --> 00:10:42.600
It's written in Ansible.

00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:46.180
It automates the tasks
like provisioning

00:10:46.180 --> 00:10:49.000
a database, provisioning
a backup, provisioning

00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:53.260
a standby database,
and hence and so forth.

00:10:53.260 --> 00:10:55.960
You can pick and choose the
amount of automation you need.

00:10:55.960 --> 00:10:58.960
You can modify this
automation if you want some

00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:00.700
features to be added from us.

00:11:00.700 --> 00:11:03.520
You can request that and
we can do that, as well.

00:11:03.520 --> 00:11:08.350
NET(net), we have the capability
to emulate any design pattern

00:11:08.350 --> 00:11:10.780
that you have today and
bring it and [INAUDIBLE]

00:11:10.780 --> 00:11:13.830
and do some extensions for you.

00:11:13.830 --> 00:11:19.080
One key aspect of any database
and deployment is storage.

00:11:19.080 --> 00:11:22.200
And we use state of the
art tier one storage.

00:11:22.200 --> 00:11:24.300
I'll talk a little bit
more about what all

00:11:24.300 --> 00:11:28.380
we do for storage and
operating systems in this.

00:11:28.380 --> 00:11:31.890
One key aspect for any
software that runs and needs

00:11:31.890 --> 00:11:36.098
an extension is that it follows
the bring your own OS model.

00:11:36.098 --> 00:11:37.890
You have [INAUDIBLE]
access to the servers.

00:11:37.890 --> 00:11:41.160
You have [INAUDIBLE]
access to that database.

00:11:41.160 --> 00:11:44.610
Google has no visibility on
what kind of applications

00:11:44.610 --> 00:11:47.100
you're running on the
Bare Metal servers.

00:11:47.100 --> 00:11:50.640
But we do make the user's
life easier by providing

00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:52.890
technologies like snapshots.

00:11:52.890 --> 00:11:58.290
So for example, today, we might
be running [INAUDIBLE] in one

00:11:58.290 --> 00:12:00.060
of the Bare Metals servers.

00:12:00.060 --> 00:12:03.300
And then you probably have
100 Bare Metals servers.

00:12:03.300 --> 00:12:07.440
And you probably want to patch
them at some point in time.

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:09.690
Since all these
servers are distressed

00:12:09.690 --> 00:12:12.570
and they're all good from a
[INAUDIBLE],, all you have to do

00:12:12.570 --> 00:12:15.720
is take a snapshot of
one of the golden images.

00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:19.350
And then roll it across
your entire fleet

00:12:19.350 --> 00:12:21.060
using the same snapshot.

00:12:21.060 --> 00:12:23.010
Hence, lowering the
amount of effort

00:12:23.010 --> 00:12:25.950
that you have to spend on
managing an operating system

00:12:25.950 --> 00:12:28.290
patch across your fleet.

00:12:28.290 --> 00:12:31.510
The same model can be
applied to applications

00:12:31.510 --> 00:12:35.420
landing on top of these
operating systems as well.

00:12:35.420 --> 00:12:37.870
Let's talk about
what Google provides

00:12:37.870 --> 00:12:39.520
as a service in this model.

00:12:39.520 --> 00:12:42.350
And what's a customer's
responsibility here?

00:12:42.350 --> 00:12:46.510
So in high level construct,
we manage anything that

00:12:46.510 --> 00:12:48.890
has to do with the hardware.

00:12:48.890 --> 00:12:51.070
So we provide the facilities.

00:12:51.070 --> 00:12:53.590
We provide the power cooling.

00:12:53.590 --> 00:12:55.240
We provide the
physical security.

00:12:55.240 --> 00:12:57.430
We manage the network security.

00:12:57.430 --> 00:13:02.780
We make sure all the
break/fix and Smart Hands

00:13:02.780 --> 00:13:05.540
is handled by us for you.

00:13:05.540 --> 00:13:08.930
At the same time, we
also tune the storage

00:13:08.930 --> 00:13:11.630
for best application
performance.

00:13:11.630 --> 00:13:15.350
We have worked with industry
standard vendors or industry

00:13:15.350 --> 00:13:16.670
leading vendors.

00:13:16.670 --> 00:13:19.130
We have taken their
best practices,

00:13:19.130 --> 00:13:22.310
combined with the best practices
of all the specialized software

00:13:22.310 --> 00:13:26.640
vendors, and implemented that
in the Bare Metal solution.

00:13:26.640 --> 00:13:29.480
So when we deprovision
a storage for you,

00:13:29.480 --> 00:13:32.750
it has been autotuned
for the next performance

00:13:32.750 --> 00:13:35.210
for that database
or the application

00:13:35.210 --> 00:13:37.670
that you're planning
to run on top of it.

00:13:37.670 --> 00:13:41.600
It takes a big
chunk of work that

00:13:41.600 --> 00:13:45.350
needed to be done when you move
from one premise to the Cloud.

00:13:45.350 --> 00:13:48.590
Again, on being the
guiding principle here,

00:13:48.590 --> 00:13:53.090
the migration to our service
running in Cloud, our goal

00:13:53.090 --> 00:13:55.640
is to minimize your
effort required

00:13:55.640 --> 00:13:59.870
to do so that you can take that
extra resources in modernizing

00:13:59.870 --> 00:14:01.817
those applications.

00:14:01.817 --> 00:14:03.900
Everything that runs in
the Bare Metal environment

00:14:03.900 --> 00:14:06.750
has access to all
Google services.

